.SH NAME
frontend/node_modules/magic-string Directory Reference
.SH SYNOPSIS
.br
.PP
.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 

.SH "magic-string"
.PP
\fR \fP \fR \fP \fR \fP

.PP
Suppose you have some source code\&. You want to make some light modifications to it - replacing a few characters here and there, wrapping it with a header and footer, etc - and ideally you'd like to generate a \fRsource map\fP at the end of it\&. You've thought about using something like \fRrecast\fP (which allows you to generate an AST from some JavaScript, manipulate it, and reprint it with a sourcemap without losing your comments and formatting), but it seems like overkill for your needs (or maybe the source code isn't JavaScript)\&.

.PP
Your requirements are, frankly, rather niche\&. But they're requirements that I also have, and for which I made magic-string\&. It's a small, fast utility for manipulating strings and generating sourcemaps\&.
.SH "Installation"
.PP
magic-string works in both node\&.js and browser environments\&. For node, install with npm:

.PP
.PP
.nf
npm i magic\-string
.fi
.PP

.PP
To use in browser, grab the \fRmagic-string\&.umd\&.js\fP file and add it to your page:

.PP
.PP
.nf
<script src="magic\-string\&.umd\&.js"></script>
.fi
.PP

.PP
(It also works with various module systems, if you prefer that sort of thing - it has a dependency on \fRvlq\fP\&.)
.SH "Usage"
.PP
These examples assume you're in node\&.js, or something similar:

.PP
.PP
.nf
import MagicString from 'magic\-string';
import fs from 'fs';

const s = new MagicString('problems = 99');

s\&.update(0, 8, 'answer');
s\&.toString(); // 'answer = 99'

s\&.update(11, 13, '42'); // character indices always refer to the original string
s\&.toString(); // 'answer = 42'

s\&.prepend('var ')\&.append(';'); // most methods are chainable
s\&.toString(); // 'var answer = 42;'

const map = s\&.generateMap({
    source: 'source\&.js',
    file: 'converted\&.js\&.map',
    includeContent: true,
}); // generates a v3 sourcemap

fs\&.writeFileSync('converted\&.js', s\&.toString());
fs\&.writeFileSync('converted\&.js\&.map', map\&.toString());
.fi
.PP

.PP
You can pass an options argument:

.PP
.PP
.nf
const s = new MagicString(someCode, {
    // these options will be used if you later call `bundle\&.addSource( s )` \- see below
    filename: 'foo\&.js',
    indentExclusionRanges: [
        /*\&.\&.\&.*/
    ],
    // mark source as ignore in DevTools, see below #Bundling
    ignoreList: false,
    // adjust the incoming position \- see below
    offset: 0,
});
.fi
.PP
.SH "Properties"
.PP
.SS "s\&.offset"
Sets the offset property to adjust the incoming position for the following APIs: \fRslice\fP, \fRupdate\fP, \fRoverwrite\fP, \fRappendLeft\fP, \fRprependLeft\fP, \fRappendRight\fP, \fRprependRight\fP, \fRmove\fP, \fRreset\fP, and \fRremove\fP\&.

.PP
Example usage:

.PP
.PP
.nf
const s = new MagicString('hello world', { offset: 0 });
s\&.offset = 6;
s\&.slice() === 'world';
.fi
.PP
.SH "Methods"
.PP
.SS "s\&.addSourcemapLocation( index )"
Adds the specified character index (with respect to the original string) to sourcemap mappings, if \fRhires\fP is \fRfalse\fP (see below)\&.
.SS "s\&.append( content )"
Appends the specified content to the end of the string\&. Returns \fRthis\fP\&.
.SS "s\&.appendLeft( index, content )"
Appends the specified \fRcontent\fP at the \fRindex\fP in the original string\&. If a range \fIending\fP with \fRindex\fP is subsequently moved, the insert will be moved with it\&. Returns \fRthis\fP\&. See also \fRs\&.prependLeft(\&.\&.\&.)\fP\&.
.SS "s\&.appendRight( index, content )"
Appends the specified \fRcontent\fP at the \fRindex\fP in the original string\&. If a range \fIstarting\fP with \fRindex\fP is subsequently moved, the insert will be moved with it\&. Returns \fRthis\fP\&. See also \fRs\&.prependRight(\&.\&.\&.)\fP\&.
.SS "s\&.clone()"
Does what you'd expect\&.
.SS "s\&.generateDecodedMap( options )"
Generates a sourcemap object with raw mappings in array form, rather than encoded as a string\&. See \fRgenerateMap\fP documentation below for options details\&. Useful if you need to manipulate the sourcemap further, but most of the time you will use \fRgenerateMap\fP instead\&.
.SS "s\&.generateMap( options )"
Generates a \fRversion 3 sourcemap\fP\&. All options are, well, optional:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRfile\fP - the filename where you plan to write the sourcemap
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRsource\fP - the filename of the file containing the original source
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRincludeContent\fP - whether to include the original content in the map's \fRsourcesContent\fP array
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRhires\fP - whether the mapping should be high-resolution\&. Hi-res mappings map every single character, meaning (for example) your devtools will always be able to pinpoint the exact location of function calls and so on\&. With lo-res mappings, devtools may only be able to identify the correct line - but they're quicker to generate and less bulky\&. You can also set \fR"boundary"\fP to generate a semi-hi-res mappings segmented per word boundary instead of per character, suitable for string semantics that are separated by words\&. If sourcemap locations have been specified with \fRs\&.addSourcemapLocation()\fP, they will be used here\&.
.PP

.PP
The returned sourcemap has two (non-enumerable) methods attached for convenience:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRtoString\fP - returns the equivalent of \fRJSON\&.stringify(map)\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRtoUrl\fP - returns a DataURI containing the sourcemap\&. Useful for doing this sort of thing:
.PP

.PP
.PP
.nf
code += '\\n//# sourceMappingURL=' + map\&.toUrl();
.fi
.PP
.SS "s\&.hasChanged()"
Indicates if the string has been changed\&.
.SS "s\&.indent( prefix[, options] )"
Prefixes each line of the string with \fRprefix\fP\&. If \fRprefix\fP is not supplied, the indentation will be guessed from the original content, falling back to a single tab character\&. Returns \fRthis\fP\&.

.PP
The \fRoptions\fP argument can have an \fRexclude\fP property, which is an array of \fR[start, end]\fP character ranges\&. These ranges will be excluded from the indentation - useful for (e\&.g\&.) multiline strings\&.
.SS "s\&.insertLeft( index, content )"
\fBDEPRECATED\fP since 0\&.17 – use \fRs\&.appendLeft(\&.\&.\&.)\fP instead
.SS "s\&.insertRight( index, content )"
\fBDEPRECATED\fP since 0\&.17 – use \fRs\&.prependRight(\&.\&.\&.)\fP instead
.SS "s\&.isEmpty()"
Returns true if the resulting source is empty (disregarding white space)\&.
.SS "s\&.locate( index )"
\fBDEPRECATED\fP since 0\&.10 – see \fR#30\fP
.SS "s\&.locateOrigin( index )"
\fBDEPRECATED\fP since 0\&.10 – see \fR#30\fP
.SS "s\&.move( start, end, index )"
Moves the characters from \fRstart\fP and \fRend\fP to \fRindex\fP\&. Returns \fRthis\fP\&.
.SS "s\&.overwrite( start, end, content[, options] )"
Replaces the characters from \fRstart\fP to \fRend\fP with \fRcontent\fP, along with the appended/prepended content in that range\&. The same restrictions as \fRs\&.remove()\fP apply\&. Returns \fRthis\fP\&.

.PP
The fourth argument is optional\&. It can have a \fRstoreName\fP property — if \fRtrue\fP, the original name will be stored for later inclusion in a sourcemap's \fRnames\fP array — and a \fRcontentOnly\fP property which determines whether only the content is overwritten, or anything that was appended/prepended to the range as well\&.

.PP
It may be preferred to use \fRs\&.update(\&.\&.\&.)\fP instead if you wish to avoid overwriting the appended/prepended content\&.
.SS "s\&.prepend( content )"
Prepends the string with the specified content\&. Returns \fRthis\fP\&.
.SS "s\&.prependLeft ( index, content )"
Same as \fRs\&.appendLeft(\&.\&.\&.)\fP, except that the inserted content will go \fIbefore\fP any previous appends or prepends at \fRindex\fP
.SS "s\&.prependRight ( index, content )"
Same as \fRs\&.appendRight(\&.\&.\&.)\fP, except that the inserted content will go \fIbefore\fP any previous appends or prepends at \fRindex\fP
.SS "s\&.replace( regexpOrString, substitution )"
String replacement with RegExp or string\&. When using a RegExp, replacer function is also supported\&. Returns \fRthis\fP\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
import MagicString from 'magic\-string';

const s = new MagicString(source);

s\&.replace('foo', 'bar');
s\&.replace(/foo/g, 'bar');
s\&.replace(/(\\w)(\\d+)/g, (_, $1, $2) => $1\&.toUpperCase() + $2);
.fi
.PP

.PP
The differences from \fR\fRString\&.replace\fP\fP:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
It will always match against the \fBoriginal string\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
It mutates the magic string state (use \fR\&.clone()\fP to be immutable)
.PP
.SS "s\&.replaceAll( regexpOrString, substitution )"
Same as \fRs\&.replace\fP, but replace all matched strings instead of just one\&. If \fRregexpOrString\fP is a regex, then it must have the global (\fRg\fP) flag set, or a \fRTypeError\fP is thrown\&. Matches the behavior of the builtin \fR\fRString\&.property\&.replaceAll\fP\fP\&. Returns \fRthis\fP\&.
.SS "s\&.remove( start, end )"
Removes the characters from \fRstart\fP to \fRend\fP (of the original string, \fBnot\fP the generated string)\&. Removing the same content twice, or making removals that partially overlap, will cause an error\&. Returns \fRthis\fP\&.
.SS "s\&.reset( start, end )"
Resets the characters from \fRstart\fP to \fRend\fP (of the original string, \fBnot\fP the generated string)\&. It can be used to restore previously removed characters and discard unwanted changes\&.
.SS "s\&.slice( start, end )"
Returns the content of the generated string that corresponds to the slice between \fRstart\fP and \fRend\fP of the original string\&. Throws error if the indices are for characters that were already removed\&.
.SS "s\&.snip( start, end )"
Returns a clone of \fRs\fP, with all content before the \fRstart\fP and \fRend\fP characters of the original string removed\&.
.SS "s\&.toString()"
Returns the generated string\&.
.SS "s\&.trim([ charType ])"
Trims content matching \fRcharType\fP (defaults to \fR\\s\fP, i\&.e\&. whitespace) from the start and end\&. Returns \fRthis\fP\&.
.SS "s\&.trimStart([ charType ])"
Trims content matching \fRcharType\fP (defaults to \fR\\s\fP, i\&.e\&. whitespace) from the start\&. Returns \fRthis\fP\&.
.SS "s\&.trimEnd([ charType ])"
Trims content matching \fRcharType\fP (defaults to \fR\\s\fP, i\&.e\&. whitespace) from the end\&. Returns \fRthis\fP\&.
.SS "s\&.trimLines()"
Removes empty lines from the start and end\&. Returns \fRthis\fP\&.
.SS "s\&.update( start, end, content[, options] )"
Replaces the characters from \fRstart\fP to \fRend\fP with \fRcontent\fP\&. The same restrictions as \fRs\&.remove()\fP apply\&. Returns \fRthis\fP\&.

.PP
The fourth argument is optional\&. It can have a \fRstoreName\fP property — if \fRtrue\fP, the original name will be stored for later inclusion in a sourcemap's \fRnames\fP array — and an \fRoverwrite\fP property which defaults to \fRfalse\fP and determines whether anything that was appended/prepended to the range will be overwritten along with the original content\&.

.PP
\fRs\&.update(start, end, content)\fP is equivalent to \fRs\&.overwrite(start, end, content, { contentOnly: true })\fP\&.
.SH "Bundling"
.PP
To concatenate several sources, use \fRMagicString\&.Bundle\fP:

.PP
.PP
.nf
const bundle = new MagicString\&.Bundle();

bundle\&.addSource({
    filename: 'foo\&.js',
    content: new MagicString('var answer = 42;'),
});

bundle\&.addSource({
    filename: 'bar\&.js',
    content: new MagicString('console\&.log( answer )'),
});

// Sources can be marked as ignore\-listed, which provides a hint to debuggers
// to not step into this code and also don't show the source files depending
// on user preferences\&.
bundle\&.addSource({
    filename: 'some\-3rdparty\-library\&.js',
    content: new MagicString('function myLib(){}'),
    ignoreList: false, // <\-\-
});

// Advanced: a source can include an `indentExclusionRanges` property
// alongside `filename` and `content`\&. This will be passed to `s\&.indent()`
// \- see documentation above

bundle
    \&.indent() // optionally, pass an indent string, otherwise it will be guessed
    \&.prepend('(function () {\\n')
    \&.append('}());');

bundle\&.toString();
// (function () {
//   var answer = 42;
//   console\&.log( answer );
// }());

// options are as per `s\&.generateMap()` above
const map = bundle\&.generateMap({
    file: 'bundle\&.js',
    includeContent: true,
    hires: true,
});
.fi
.PP

.PP
As an alternative syntax, if you a) don't have \fRfilename\fP or \fRindentExclusionRanges\fP options, or b) passed those in when you used \fRnew MagicString(\&.\&.\&.)\fP, you can simply pass the \fRMagicString\fP instance itself:

.PP
.PP
.nf
const bundle = new MagicString\&.Bundle();
const source = new MagicString(someCode, {
    filename: 'foo\&.js',
});

bundle\&.addSource(source);
.fi
.PP
